From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 08:07:37 +0300
Subject: nv30: big texture uploads reuse staging buffers too
 (NV30_STAGING_POOL_MB)

OpenTTD's OpenGL driver draws the screen on the CPU into a pixel buffer and uploads the dirty
rectangle with glTexSubImage2D every frame: the whole 1080p screen (8.3 MB) and its 2 MB
palette animation buffer at the title screen. Mesa's PBO upload on nv30 goes through
nv30_miptree_transfer_map, and every map made a new GART staging buffer of the box's size: on
the PS3 a GEM create, which zeroes the memory (memset 5.3% of the game's samples), an mmap,
one hash page table fault per 4 KiB page on the first write (each an lv1 insertion with
interrupts soft-masked: 26% of the samples, all of it shown as __replay_soft_interrupts), and,
once the copy's fence signalled, a munmap with one lv1 invalidation per page (8.2%) and a GEM
close. About 40% of the game's CPU. Each of those buffers is over 4 MB, so
nv30_staging_bo_new() also waited for the GPU before every one of them.

With NV30_STAGING_POOL_MB=M (0, the default: off; at most 64) write-only uploads over 256 KiB
(the NV30_STAGING_POOL limit) take a buffer of their MiB class, or one up to twice its size,
from a pool of idle buffers that are still mapped, and the fence work that freed the buffer
gives it back instead, as NV30_STAGING_POOL does for small ones. The pool holds at most M MiB
and 16 buffers, oldest given back first, and every buffer unused for a second goes back to the
kernel (checked at every flush), so a burst of uploads at a load leaves no GART behind. A
reused buffer takes no new GART, so it is not counted in staging_inflight and never waits.
NV30_STAGING_POOL_STATS=1 prints the pool's hits and misses when the screen goes away.

The decisions are in nv30_staging_policy.h, with no Mesa types, for the host test
(mesa/tests/staging-pool-test.c in the PS3 tree).

Forwarded: no

